// The Jade half of ovata-infer-protocol — what an inference provider receives,
// and what it may hand back.
//
// This file is the single definition both sides are written against. A Jade
// engine constructs an `InferRequest` and calls a provider package's `infer`; the
// package returns an array of the frames declared below.
//
// A rename here is a rename both consumers see. The Jade compiler cannot import
// a `.jde` into its own Rust and C sources, so it carries a hand-written copy of
// these names — and a tripwire test there (`src/llm/tests.rs` in the jade repo)
// parses this file and fails the build until the copy follows.
//
// Provider packages reach this by registering `jade/` as a [lib] in jade.toml:
//
//     [lib.ovata]
//     path = "path/to/ovata-infer-protocol/jade"
//
// then `use ovata::infer`.

// ── Request ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The first field is `input`, not `prompt`: `prompt` is a Jade keyword and
// cannot name a struct field, and `input` is the better word here anyway — what
// a provider receives is the text to complete, not a Jade prompt binding.

// One inference call. `input` is always set; the rest are nil unless the call
// carries a grammar constraint. `anchor` and `stop_anchor` are only meaningful
// alongside `grammar` — they bound the span it constrains.
struct InferRequest {
    let input = ""
    let grammar = nil
    let anchor = nil
    let stop_anchor = nil
}

// ── Response ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `infer` returns an array of frames. A normal reply is any number of `Token`s
// followed by a `Done`; a failure is a single `Error`, which the caller raises as
// a catchable Jade error.
//
// Each frame may be written two ways, and a caller accepts both:
//
//     Token { text: "hi" }              // the struct form
//     {"type": "Token", "text": "hi"}   // the dict form
//
// They are the same frame. The struct's *type name* is what the dict spells in
// its `"type"` key, so the two forms carry identical information — the struct
// just gets it checked. The dict form came first and stays supported; new
// providers should prefer the struct form, since a typo in a struct name is a
// compile error where a typo in a dict key is a frame the caller cannot read.
//
// Anything else in the array is a protocol violation and the caller raises. It
// used to skip what it did not recognise, which turned a renamed key or a
// miscased tag into an empty reply — the model appearing to say nothing, with no
// error anywhere. A loud failure is the point of declaring these.

// One piece of generated text. A reply is the concatenation of its `Token`s in
// order.
struct Token {
    let text = ""
}

// Generation finished normally. Carries the total tokens billed for the request.
struct Done {
    let tokens_used = 0
}

// Generation failed, and no further frames follow. The caller turns this into a
// catchable Jade error carrying `message`, so a `?p` inside a `try` can catch it.
struct Error {
    let message = ""
}

// Names the provider that served the reply — an opaque label the protocol does
// not interpret, sent ahead of any tokens. Contributes no text.
struct Meta {
    let provider = ""
}

// An out-of-band structured payload, as a JSON string: a tool call, a health
// report, anything whose schema the eliciting request defines. Contributes no
// plain text, so a caller reading a reply as a string ignores it.
struct Json {
    let json = ""
}
